Report the app's lifecycle to Flutter (needs 0007, 0010).

Lipstick tells the main window how it's shown through the Qt surface
extension's onscreen_visibility: 5 in front, 3 only as the app's cover
(its thumbnail in the tile), 0 not at all (minimised behind a cover window,
screen off, another app in front). These are now passed on as Flutter's app
lifecycle on flutter/lifecycle: 5 resumed, 3 hidden, 0 paused, and resumed
at start-up, since Lipstick only reports changes. The framework fills in the
states between (inactive, hidden).

So apps in the background stop drawing frames and running animations, and
can react (save state, pause playback). Covers keep updating while the app
is paused, since they're drawn off-screen.

There's no "visible but not active" value: a cancelled edge swipe doesn't
change the main window's visibility.

diff --git a/src/flutter/shell/platform/linux_embedded/flutter_elinux_view.cc b/src/flutter/shell/platform/linux_embedded/flutter_elinux_view.cc
index fa34e68..c55f4d5 100644
--- a/src/flutter/shell/platform/linux_embedded/flutter_elinux_view.cc
+++ b/src/flutter/shell/platform/linux_embedded/flutter_elinux_view.cc
@@ -140,6 +140,29 @@ void FlutterELinuxView::OnContentOrientationChanged() {
   }
 }
 
+void FlutterELinuxView::OnAppVisibilityChanged(AppVisibility visibility) {
+  if (!lifecycle_handler_ || app_visibility_ == visibility) {
+    return;
+  }
+  app_visibility_ = visibility;
+  // The framework fills in the states between, e.g. inactive and hidden
+  // between resumed and paused.
+  switch (visibility) {
+    case AppVisibility::kForeground:
+      ELINUX_LOG(INFO) << "App lifecycle: resumed";
+      lifecycle_handler_->OnResumed();
+      break;
+    case AppVisibility::kThumbnail:
+      ELINUX_LOG(INFO) << "App lifecycle: hidden";
+      lifecycle_handler_->OnHidden();
+      break;
+    case AppVisibility::kBackground:
+      ELINUX_LOG(INFO) << "App lifecycle: paused";
+      lifecycle_handler_->OnPaused();
+      break;
+  }
+}
